- How does the solution change if edge weights represent communication delay instead of task duration?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-key streaming stats with independent sliding windows, combining deques, hashtables, and a heap with lazy expirations. It mirrors real services that analyze per-entity signals under time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key deques for time-bounded data
- Hash map for key -> stats
- Max-heap with lazy deletion to keep top averages up-to-date
- Expiration handling driven by currentTime

## Code Example
```javascript
class PerKeyRollingStats {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.map = new Map(); this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  add(key, value, ts) {
    let rec = this.map.get(key) || { dq: [], sum: 0 };
    rec.dq.push([ts, value]);
    rec.sum += value;
    while (rec.dq.length && rec.dq[0][0] < ts - this.W) {
      const [t, v] = rec.dq.shift();
      rec.sum -= v;
    }
    this.map.set(key, rec);
    const avg = rec.sum / rec.dq.length;
    this.heap.push({ key, avg, validAt: ts, len: rec.dq.length });
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const rec = this.map.get(top.key);
      const avg = rec && rec.dq.length ? rec.sum / rec.dq.length : -Infinity;
      if (top.avg !== avg) continue; // stale entry
      res.push(top.key);
    }
    // optional: reinsert from a temp to preserve heap state
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a very large number of keys with memory constraints?
- How would you adapt to variable window sizes per key or out-of-order events?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests practical DP skills: counting subsets, managing zeros, and comparing memoization vs tabulation.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting
- Top-down memoization
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and zero handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsetsTopDown(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Map());
  function dfs(i, t){
    if (t === 0) return 1;
    if (i === n) return 0;
    const key = t;
    if (memo[i].has(key)) return memo[i].get(key);
    let res = dfs(i+1, t);
    if (t >= nums[i]) res = (res + dfs(i+1, t - nums[i]));
    memo[i].set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}
function countSubsetsBottomUp(nums, target){
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const v of nums){
    for (let w = target; w >= v; w--){
      dp[w] = (dp[w] + dp[w - v]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to handle negative numbers or count modulo large M?
- How would you modify to return counts for multiple target sums simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem demonstrates how to model a constrained, single-use resource within a shortest-path framework. It tests understanding of state-space expansion and whether a candidate can adapt classic algorithms to modified constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **State-Space Expansion:** Augment each vertex with a binary flag indicating coupon usage
- **Layer 0 (coupon unused):** Can transition to layer 0 (full cost) or layer 1 (half cost)
- **Layer 1 (coupon used):** Can only transition to layer 1 (full cost only)
- **Path Reconstruction:** Track predecessor states to identify which edge receives the discount

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithCoupon(graph, s, t) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [Infinity, Infinity]);
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [null, null]);
  const pq = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => a.dist < b.dist);

  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.push({ u: s, used: 0, dist: 0 });

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const { u, used, dist: d } = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if (u === t) break;

    for (const { v, w } of graph[u]) {
      // Transition without using coupon
      if (d + w < dist[v][used]) {
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = { u, used };
        pq.push({ u: v, used, dist: dist[v][used] });
      }
      // Transition using coupon (only if not yet used)
      if (used === 0) {
        const newDist = d + w / 2;
        if (newDist < dist[v][1]) {
          dist[v][1] = newDist;
          prev[v][1] = { u, used, discounted: { from: u, to: v, weight: w } };
          pq.push({ u: v, used: 1, dist: newDist });
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const best = dist[t][0] < dist[t][1] ? 0 : 1;
  return { distance: dist[t][best], finalState: best };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to k coupons instead of one?
- How would you allow the discount on any one edge (not just along a shortest path)?
- How does this change if the coupon provides a fixed discount rather than percentage reduction?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to apply k-way merge algorithms to partially sorted data, a common scenario in data processing pipelines and external sorting. It emphasizes understanding how the number of runs (r) impacts both time complexity and memory usage, connecting theoretical concepts with practical implementation.

## Key Concepts

- **k-way merge**: Efficient merging of multiple sorted sequences
- **Run detection**: Identifying sorted segments in a single linear pass
- **Min-heap**: Priority queue for selecting minimum elements efficiently
- **Complexity analysis**: O(n log r) time, O(r) auxiliary space
- **Edge cases**: Empty input, duplicate elements, and single-run scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor() { 
    this.heap = []; 
  }

  size() { 
    return this.heap.length; 
  }

  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this._heapifyUp(this.heap.length - 1);
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return null;
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const last = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = last;
      this._heapifyDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }

  _heapifyUp(index) { /* implementation */ }
  _heapifyDown(index) { /* implementation */ }
}

function kWayMerge(arr) {
  // Edge case: empty input
  if (arr.length === 0) return [];

  // Detect runs
  const runs = [];
  let start = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    if (i === arr.length || arr[i-1] > arr[i]) {
      runs.push({start, end: i-1, index: start});
      start = i;
    }
  }

  // Edge case: r=1 (already sorted)
  if (runs.length === 1) return arr.slice();

  // Initialize heap with first elements
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  const result = [];

  runs.forEach((run, i) => {
    heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId: i});
  });

  // Merge process
  while (heap.size() > 0) {
    const {value, runId} = heap.pop();
    result.push(value);

    const run = runs[runId];
    if (run.index < run.end) {
      run.index++;
      heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId});
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

